= Solution
{parent=a}

Use $[a_i,a_j^\dagger]=\delta_{ij}$ and define $E_{ij}=a_i^\dagger a_j$. A direct multiplication gives
$$
[E_{ij},E_{kl}]=\delta_{jk}E_{il}-\delta_{il}E_{kj}.
$$
With $S_+=\hbar a_\uparrow^\dagger a_\downarrow$, $S_-=\hbar a_\downarrow^\dagger a_\uparrow$, this implies $[S_+,S_-]=2\hbar S_z$ and $[S_z,S_\pm]=\pm\hbar S_\pm$. Resolving the raising and lowering combinations gives
$$
\boxed{[S_i,S_j]=i\hbar\varepsilon_{ijk}S_k}.
$$
These operators realize the <Schwinger boson representation> of <angular momentum>. Each commutes with $\widehat N=n_\uparrow+n_\downarrow$, so fixed total occupation is an invariant subspace.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The bosonic <commutator> is important in $S_+S_-=\hbar^2n_\uparrow(n_\downarrow+1)$ and $S_-S_+=\hbar^2n_\downarrow(n_\uparrow+1)$. Therefore
$$
S^2=S_z^2+\frac{S_+S_-+S_-S_+}{2}
=\frac{\hbar^2}{4}\left[(n_\uparrow+n_\downarrow)^2+2(n_\uparrow+n_\downarrow)\right].
$$
On the $N$-particle sector,
$$
\boxed{S^2=\hbar^2\frac N2\left(\frac N2+1\right),\qquad s=\frac N2}.
$$
For example $N=0,1,2$ gives <spin> $0,\tfrac12,1$. The total <number operator> can be retained in the same identity before restricting to a sector.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Total symmetry makes a component depend only on its number $n_\uparrow$ of up indices. The possible values are $0,1,\ldots,N$, giving
$$
\boxed{\dim\operatorname{Sym}^N(\mathbb C^2)=N+1=2s+1}.
$$
A normalized basis is $|n_\uparrow,N-n_\uparrow\rangle$, with $S_z$ <eigenvalue> $\hbar(n_\uparrow-N/2)$. It is precisely one spin-$N/2$ multiplet. These are independent complex amplitudes before normalization and removal of a common <quantum phase>, rather than $N+1$ independent real physical parameters.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The defining matrix conditions are
$$
\boxed{SU(2)=\{U\in M_2(\mathbb C):U^\dagger U=I,\ \det U=1\}}.
$$
The group operation is matrix multiplication. Equivalently $U=a_0I-i\mathbf a\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma$ with real $a_0,\mathbf a$ and $a_0^2+|\mathbf a|^2=1$, so it has three real parameters and is a compact connected simply connected <Lie group>. Its Hermitian <spin> generators obey the <commutator> from part (a); the corresponding Lie-algebra matrices themselves are traceless and anti-Hermitian.

= e
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

**The literal printed difference is zero**, because the same spin-index product appears twice, and zero is invariant under every transformation. The intended <SU(2)-invariant antisymmetric spinor contraction> uses opposite indices in the second term. With $\varepsilon=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}$, define $I=\phi^T\varepsilon\chi$. For any two-by-two matrix,
$$
U^T\varepsilon U=(\det U)\varepsilon.
$$
Thus for $U\in SU(2)$,
$$
\boxed{I'=(U\phi)^T\varepsilon(U\chi)=I}.
$$
Expanding the <determinant> identity proves the result without complex conjugating either <spinor>. This is the invariant alternating tensor behind the <spin singlet state>. If the two <spinors> are identical, the contraction vanishes. An antisymmetric internal two-boson state also needs an antisymmetric additional degree of freedom for overall bosonic symmetry; it does not lie in the fully symmetric fixed-two-mode sector discussed above.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A constant electrostatic <energy> offset is removed locally by the <quantum phase> substitution $\psi=e^{-iV_0t/\hbar}\psi_0$, since $(i\hbar\partial_t-V_0)\psi=e^{-iV_0t/\hbar}i\hbar\partial_t\psi_0$. Therefore the two <frequency> branches become
$$
\boxed{\psi=\sum_k\frac1{\sqrt{2\omega_k}}\left[
a_ke^{ikx-i(\omega_k+V_0/\hbar)t}
+b_k^\dagger e^{-ikx+i(\omega_k-V_0/\hbar)t}\right]}.
$$
The normalization uses the kinetic <frequency> $\omega_k$, while the canonical <energy> <eigenvalues> are $E=V_0\pm\hbar\omega_k$. The opposite-frequency branch is not obtained by replacing $\omega_k$ by the same shifted positive <frequency> in both terms. A globally constant offset produces no <electric field>, whereas different offsets on opposite sides of a step cannot be removed by a single smooth <global phase>.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Assume a positive-energy incoming particle, $E>mc^2$, and a nonzero upward step $V_0>0$. Set $M=mc^2$ and $k=\sqrt{E^2-M^2}/(\hbar c)>0$. With the common time factor $e^{-iEt/\hbar}$ removed, take $\phi_<(x)=e^{ikx}+r e^{-ikx}$ and $\phi_>(x)=t e^{iqx}$. The right-hand wavenumber satisfies $q^2=[(E-V_0)^2-M^2]/(\hbar c)^2$; its physical branch is specified in part (c). Integrating the stationary equation across the finite step requires continuity of $\phi$ and $\phi'$, giving $1+r=t$ and $k(1-r)=qt$. Thus
$$
\boxed{r=\frac{k-q}{k+q},\qquad t=\frac{2k}{k+q}}.
$$
These are wave amplitudes, not <flux> probabilities. The <Noether current of scalar electrodynamics>, in a convenient common normalization for a stationary mode, is $\rho_Q=(E-V)|\phi|^2/(mc^2)$ and $j_Q=(\hbar/m)\operatorname{Im}(\phi^*\partial_x\phi)$. For a real $q$, its charge-current ratios are $\mathcal R=|r|^2$ and $\mathcal T=(q/k)|t|^2$, with $\mathcal R+\mathcal T=1$. The same statement uses $\operatorname{Re}q$ for a decaying solution. The <electrostatic potential> couples through the time derivative; it is not a Lorentz-scalar <mass> shift. A zero step is recovered separately with $q=k$, $r=0$ and $t=1$.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $z=E-V_0+i0$. The <outgoing analytic branch of Klein-Gordon step momentum> is
$$
\boxed{q(z)=\frac{\sqrt{z-M}\sqrt{z+M}}{\hbar c}},
$$
where each <square root> is continued from the <upper half-plane>. It behaves as $z/(\hbar c)$ at large positive or negative real $z$. The <branch points> are $E=V_0\pm M$; replacing this product by a principal <square root> of $z^2-M^2$ can choose the wrong sign below the lower threshold.

**I.** For $E-V_0>M$, $q>0$ and both transmitted <kinetic energy> and <group velocity> are positive. Thus $0\leq\mathcal R<1$ and $\mathcal T=4kq/(k+q)^2>0$; the transmission decreases to zero on approaching the upper threshold from above.

**II.** For $|E-V_0|<M$, write $q=i\kappa$ with $\kappa=\sqrt{M^2-(E-V_0)^2}/(\hbar c)>0$. The right wave decays, $r=(k-i\kappa)/(k+i\kappa)$ has modulus one, and $\mathcal T=0$. There is an evanescent tail but no <flux> transmitted to infinity. Its reflection <quantum phase> changes continuously between the two thresholds.

**III.** For $E-V_0<-M$, the <analytic continuation> gives $q=-Q$, where $Q=\sqrt{(E-V_0)^2-M^2}/(\hbar c)>0$. The <group velocity> $c^2\hbar q/(E-V_0)$ is again rightward, while the charge density and transmitted charge current are negative. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathcal R=\frac{(k+Q)^2}{(k-Q)^2}>1,\qquad
\mathcal T=-\frac{4kQ}{(k-Q)^2}<0,\qquad
\mathcal R+\mathcal T=1}.
$$
Choosing $q=+Q$ would describe a wavepacket arriving from the right, inconsistent with the chosen incoming channel. As the lower threshold is approached, $q\to0^-$ and $\mathcal R\to1$ from above. This <Klein-Gordon step amplification> has a real denominator zero at $E=V_0/2$ when $V_0>2M$: there $Q=k$, and no finite stationary solution with unit incoming amplitude satisfies both matching equations. The resulting formal pole must not be described as an infinite single-particle <probability>. Finite switching, wavepackets and the treatment of the external source require a multiparticle analysis.<Klein Paradox for Spin-0 Particles by Douglas M. Gingrich>[Equations 4.142–4.155: outgoing negative-momentum branch, boundary matching and charge currents]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-klein-step-branches.png]
{title=Original outgoing Klein-Gordon momentum branches and charge-current reflection for a supercritical sharp step}

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The negative-kinetic-energy branch is interpreted as an <antiparticle> channel. In the overlap $mc^2<E<V_0-mc^2$, particles can escape to the left and oppositely charged <antiparticles> to the right. This interval exists only when $V_0>2mc^2$. The external electrostatic source supplies the <energy> for pair creation; the reflected particle <flux> can exceed the incident one because the outgoing state need not contain only the incident particle.

**The conserved quantity is signed charge current, not a positive one-particle <probability> in both regions.** Negative $\mathcal T$ denotes rightward transport of opposite charge, so it is consistent with $\mathcal R>1$ and $\mathcal R+\mathcal T=1$. A quantized charged field with particle and <antiparticle> creation operators gives the appropriate interpretation of this <Klein paradox>. This bosonic result must not be replaced by a fermionic reflection law or interpreted as a failure of charge conservation.<Klein Paradox for Spin-0 Particles by Douglas M. Gingrich>[Discussion after equation 4.155: pair creation and the oppositely signed transmitted current]{ref}
